# Check every text file out with LF, on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites `.py`, `.md`, `.yaml` and `.json`
# to CRLF. That matters here because this CLI hashes text: managed integration files are compared by
# byte hash to decide whether the user edited them, and workspace artifacts are fingerprinted the
# same way. A CRLF checkout made both read as "changed" on a machine that had changed nothing.
#
# It also keeps `scripts/install.sh` usable: that file is piped straight into `sh` from the landing
# page, and `sh` chokes on a CRLF shebang line.
#
# The CLI pins its own writes to LF separately (workspace/textio.py), because it writes into trees it
# did not check out. The two are complementary: this fixes the checkout, that fixes everything else.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary payloads git must never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.whl binary
